# Entity, device and area pickers

A picker decides what an action will affect. Keep the native picker for entities, devices, areas, floors, labels and targets. A plain text field or a fixed list is not a replacement for a searchable picker that uses Home Assistant's registry and filters.

This is reference specification HA-CTRL-008. The examples are simulated. They do not connect to Home Assistant or control a home.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-008-capabilities">What each selection means</h2>

| Selection | What to keep |
| --- | --- |
| Entity | The stable entity ID and the native domain and capability filters. Friendly names can be the same for different entities. |
| Device | A device can have several entities with different capabilities. It is not the same as one entity. |
| Area, floor or label | The native grouping and how it resolves. Explain that changing who belongs to the group can change what is affected. |
| Several items | Each item, a useful count and a way to remove each item by keyboard. The order of selection must not change what is meant. |
| Action target | The whole supported target, including exclusions. Do not pull out only the entity IDs and drop the rest. |

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-008-states">Empty results, saved targets and failure</h2>

Say which of these happened: no search matches, no compatible targets, results limited by permission or a failed registry request. “No devices” is wrong if a filter is hiding them. Do not show names the account is not allowed to see.

A saved entity can be unavailable and still exist. A deleted target needs an unresolved state and a clear choice about how to repair it. Do not choose the first result for the person. Do not remove an inaccessible target when the page loads. When a filter changes, show how already chosen values are affected before saving.

Show friendly names as supplied, with their punctuation and language. Where names repeat, add the area, domain or a stable ID to tell them apart. Keep exclusions and mixed target types when the value is saved. The action's target is not a list of display labels.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-008-content">Wording</h2>

Label the question by its purpose, for example “Which lights should this action affect?”. Show the filter scope beside the search. A count means the matching set known now. It does not promise every member is available, or allowed, when the action runs. Removal wording must say whether it takes a target out of this draft or deletes an entity from Home Assistant. These are different operations.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-008-accessibility">Keyboard and screen reader use</h2>

Keep the native combobox and listbox behaviour and keyboard navigation of results. Do not add those roles to a partial copy. Announce changes to results and selections without reading every option again. A remove control for a chosen item needs the item's name and a predictable place for focus to go. At larger text sizes, show the detail that tells duplicates apart instead of clipping them all the same way.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-008-integration">Keep the native picker</h2>

Use native selectors with their allowed domain, integration, device class and supported feature filters. These examples are specimens for review and naming, not a new picker. In a blueprint or action, keep the native target value whole. Check the installed frontend's selector behaviour and its backend validation. A successful visual selection is not authorisation. For other selector types, see [Schema selectors and configuration forms](schema-selectors.html).

